  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 320 PM Friday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A few low pressure systems should move north \nthrough or near the region this weekend, this will bring a chance of \nwidespread rain. High uncertainty remains in the location and amount \nof heavier rainfall. \n\nThere is currently a stationary front bisecting Florida. Multiple \nlow pressure systems look to form along the front and move north. \nThe location and tracks of the low(s) differ greatly between the \nmodels leading to high uncertainty in the forecast for this weekend. \nWhile the probability of at least measurable rain (0.01 in) looks \nhigh over the entire region (75%+), the spread in the exact amount \nis also very high. Rain looks to begin as early as Saturday evening \nand should exit the region late Sunday night. The biggest question \nmarks look to be where the low that is currently off the FL coast \ntracks and how the low that is expected to form further east will \ninteract with it. Both of these will determine where the heaviest \nrainfall will track. Currently, it looks like there could be a swath \nof rainfall in the 1 to 2 inch range somewhere within the CWA, \nhowever models are struggling with the location (whether it occurs \nin the Piedmont/Triad or the Coastal Plain). The secondary more \neastern low should get stronger off the NC coast as it moves \nnorthward. Depending on the strength and location the low tracks, \nthis may deprive portions of central NC moisture and instability, \nreducing rainfall totals. If this low tracks closer to the coast, it \ncould also increase rainfall totals, especially in the east. As the \nlows continue to develop and evolve, models should be able to get a \nbetter handle on things. \n\nThe mean and 50th percentiles of the REFS suggest that the largest \nvalues of CAPE should remain confined close to the coast. This would \nmean that the severe weather risk will remain low. This is also \nsupported by the AI and analog severe models which show a low to \nzero chance of severe weather. However, don't want to completely \nrule out a few stronger storms. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Another round of rain is possible from mid to late \nnext week, along with much cooler temperatures.\n\nA backdoor cold front will move south through the area on Monday \nnight and early Tuesday, with much cooler air behind it for the rest \nof the workweek as Canadian high pressure builds in. A CAD pattern \nmay develop with the strong NE flow. While current forecast highs \nare in the 50s from Wednesday through Friday, raw guidance indicates \ntemperatures may struggle to get out of the 40s over the Piedmont. \n\nMeanwhile, surface low pressure will develop off the Southeast US \ncoast as a shortwave moves east from the Southern Plains. While rain \nappears likely across central NC sometime from Wednesday through \nFriday, there is still a lot of uncertainty on amounts as well as \ntiming. At this time the best chance for rain looks to be late \nWednesday into early Thursday, which is supported by the ECMWF and \nCMC. But the 12z GFS holds back the shortwave and develops it into a \nclosed low that takes its time moving east, which wouldn't bring the \nbest chance of rain to the area until Friday. Regardless, looking \nlike a chilly, cloudy, and potentially rainy Thanksgiving is in \nstore.",
